1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,037
This video explains what a compiler is, and the five phases that every compiler has.

2
00:00:06,487 --> 00:00:21,293
First we look at programming languages and how they are implemented. Then we follow one small program through the five phases: lexical analysis, parsing, semantic analysis, optimization and code generation.

3
00:00:21,743 --> 00:00:26,415
This course is about compiler construction. You learn two kinds of things.

4
00:00:26,865 --> 00:00:37,979
First, formal language theory. For example, regular languages and context-free languages. These give exact descriptions of what a program may look like.

5
00:00:38,429 --> 00:00:53,405
Second, the stages of a modern compiler. Lexical analysis, grammars and parsing. Type checking. Static analysis. Code optimization. Code generation. Memory management and runtime support.

6
00:00:53,855 --> 00:00:56,309
The theory tells you what is possible.

7
00:00:56,759 --> 00:00:59,020
The stages tell you how to build it.

8
00:00:59,470 --> 00:01:07,491
A programming language is a precise mathematical construct that lets you program a computer. It has two aspects.

9
00:01:07,941 --> 00:01:17,605
The first aspect is syntax. Syntax is the set of language constructs, for example if-else and while. It is the form of the program.

10
00:01:18,055 --> 00:01:27,591
The second aspect is semantics: what the constructs mean. Here, if x is greater than 0, y becomes 1. Else y becomes 2.

11
00:01:28,041 --> 00:01:35,615
Semantics assumes a model of computation, for example Turing machines or mathematical functions.

12
00:01:36,065 --> 00:01:42,337
There are three styles to define semantics: denotational, operational and axiomatic.

13
00:01:42,787 --> 00:01:49,635
Languages fall into two large groups. In a declarative language, you specify what to compute.

14
00:01:50,085 --> 00:01:52,858
The run-time and the compiler find out how.

15
00:01:53,308 --> 00:01:55,868
Examples are SQL and Matlab.

16
00:01:56,318 --> 00:02:03,721
Pure functional languages, such as Haskell, and logic-based languages, such as Prolog, are declarative.

17
00:02:04,171 --> 00:02:09,653
In an imperative language, you specify both what to compute and how to compute it.

18
00:02:10,103 --> 00:02:13,751
Examples are C, C plus plus, Java and Python.

19
00:02:14,201 --> 00:02:23,375
Most languages are declarative, imperative or mixed. You can add features such as object orientation to any of these groups.

20
00:02:23,825 --> 00:02:42,278
Programming language design is not easy. A language must do many things. It must let programmers make efficient code. It must help them build secure and correct programs. It must discourage bad practices, be easy to use, and automate routine tasks.

21
00:02:42,728 --> 00:02:49,469
These goals pull against each other. The course notes use the example of automatic array bounds checking.

22
00:02:49,919 --> 00:02:52,821
It costs the compiler engineer time and thought.

23
00:02:53,271 --> 00:02:55,511
The compiler runs a little longer.

24
00:02:55,961 --> 00:03:02,617
The executable program is a bit slower and a bit bigger, because it must store the size of every array.

25
00:03:03,067 --> 00:03:05,328
But the programmer saves time and thought.

26
00:03:05,778 --> 00:03:09,426
And the end user is safe from buffer overflow attacks.

27
00:03:09,876 --> 00:03:18,580
Every language feature is a trade-off like this. It has costs for some people and gains for others. This is a theme of the course.

28
00:03:19,030 --> 00:03:24,555
How is a language implemented? There are two major strategies, and a mix of the two.

29
00:03:25,005 --> 00:03:30,211
An interpreter runs the program as is, with little or no preprocessing.

30
00:03:30,661 --> 00:03:34,501
It takes the program and the input, and it gives the output.

31
00:03:34,951 --> 00:03:37,575
An interpreter is a virtual machine.

32
00:03:38,025 --> 00:03:43,465
JavaScript, TypeScript, Python and Java bytecode are mostly interpreted.

33
00:03:43,915 --> 00:03:50,976
A compiler does extensive preprocessing and offline optimization first. It makes an executable.

34
00:03:51,426 --> 00:03:55,735
Later, the executable runs on the input, and it gives the output.

35
00:03:56,185 --> 00:04:00,879
Batch compilation systems such as gcc and clang dominate.

36
00:04:01,329 --> 00:04:05,873
The mixed strategy is just-in-time compilation, or JIT.

37
00:04:06,323 --> 00:04:13,299
Some environments, such as Lisp, give both: an interpreter for development and a compiler for production.

38
00:04:13,749 --> 00:04:23,114
What is a compiler? A compiler is a program that translates a program, usually in a high-level language, into a lower-level language.

39
00:04:23,564 --> 00:04:28,407
Here is the small program x equals 2 plus 3, from the course notes.

40
00:04:28,857 --> 00:04:35,833
First the compiler parses the string into a tree. This is the abstract syntax tree, or AST.

41
00:04:36,283 --> 00:04:38,373
The root of the tree is the assignment.

42
00:04:38,823 --> 00:04:40,637
Its left child is x.

43
00:04:41,087 --> 00:04:44,863
Its right child is the plus, with 2 and 3 below it.

44
00:04:45,313 --> 00:04:50,390
Then code generation turns the tree into assembly code. Four instructions.

45
00:04:50,840 --> 00:04:53,805
Run them. Move 2 into register e a x.

46
00:04:54,255 --> 00:04:56,837
Move 3 into register e b x.

47
00:04:57,287 --> 00:05:01,553
Add e b x to e a x. Now e a x holds 5.

48
00:05:02,003 --> 00:05:06,099
Push e a x. The stack holds 5, which is 2 plus 3.

49
00:05:06,549 --> 00:05:15,915
So the compiler turns a string into a tree, and the tree into another string. The output program must do the same thing as the input program.

50
00:05:16,365 --> 00:05:23,810
On the way, the compiler also checks the program for errors, optimizes it, and links it to code libraries.

51
00:05:24,260 --> 00:05:30,916
Some history. In 1954, IBM developed the 704 computer.

52
00:05:31,366 --> 00:05:38,214
There was a problem. Software costs were higher than hardware costs. All programming was done in assembly.

53
00:05:38,664 --> 00:05:46,408
John Backus had an idea: translate high-level code to assembly. Many people thought that this was impossible.

54
00:05:46,858 --> 00:05:56,245
The FORTRAN I project ran from 1954 to 1957. FORTRAN means formula translation.

55
00:05:56,695 --> 00:06:02,177
By 1958, more than 50 percent of all software was in FORTRAN.

56
00:06:02,627 --> 00:06:07,065
FORTRAN I was the first compiler. Development time was halved.

57
00:06:07,515 --> 00:06:11,525
And execution efficiency was only 20 percent lower.

58
00:06:11,975 --> 00:06:18,013
FORTRAN I led to a lot of theoretical work, and modern compilers still keep its outline.

59
00:06:18,463 --> 00:06:26,996
A compiler is a linear pipeline. Each phase makes the data for the next phase. There are five phases, numbered 1 to 5.

60
00:06:27,446 --> 00:06:35,553
Phase 1 is lexical analysis. The lexical analyzer reads the input program and makes a stream of tokens.

61
00:06:36,003 --> 00:06:43,256
Phase 2 is parsing. The parser reads the tokens and builds the abstract syntax tree, the AST.

62
00:06:43,706 --> 00:06:52,687
Phase 3 is semantic analysis, for example type checking. It produces an intermediate representation, or IR.

63
00:06:53,137 --> 00:06:59,345
Phase 4 is optimization. It changes the IR, here into three-address format.

64
00:06:59,795 --> 00:07:04,275
Phase 5 is code generation. It writes the output program.

65
00:07:04,725 --> 00:07:15,691
We can understand the first three phases by comparison with the way people understand a natural language, such as English. We now go through the phases one by one.

66
00:07:16,141 --> 00:07:23,714
Phase 1. When you read, the first step is to recognize words. A word is the smallest unit above letters.

67
00:07:24,164 --> 00:07:27,236
This is a sentence has four words and a period.

68
00:07:27,686 --> 00:07:41,574
The lexical analyzer does the same with program text. It divides the text into words, called tokens. Here is the line: if x equals equals y then z equals 1, else z equals 2.

69
00:07:42,024 --> 00:07:48,339
Each token has a unit, shown by colour. The keywords are if, then and else: 3 keywords.

70
00:07:48,789 --> 00:07:53,247
The variables are x, y, and z two times: 4 variables.

71
00:07:53,697 --> 00:07:59,137
The operators are the equals equals and the two equals signs: 3 operators.

72
00:07:59,587 --> 00:08:01,443
The constants are 1 and 2.

73
00:08:01,893 --> 00:08:04,731
The punctuation is the two semicolons.

74
00:08:05,181 --> 00:08:11,965
The spaces between the tokens are white space. There are 11 of them. White space is also a unit.

75
00:08:12,415 --> 00:08:22,697
So the lexer finds 3 plus 4 plus 3 plus 2 plus 2, which is 14 tokens. Video 2 shows exactly how the lexer finds them.

76
00:08:23,147 --> 00:08:34,305
Phase 2. When the words are known, the next step is to understand the structure of the sentence. Parsing is diagramming sentences, and the diagram is a tree.

77
00:08:34,755 --> 00:08:44,803
Here is the sentence: This line is a long sentence. First, each word gets its category: article, noun, verb, article, adjective, noun.

78
00:08:45,253 --> 00:08:48,645
Then the words are grouped. This line is the subject.

79
00:08:49,095 --> 00:08:51,228
A long sentence is the object.

80
00:08:51,678 --> 00:08:56,905
Subject, verb and object together make the sentence. That is the root of the tree.

81
00:08:57,355 --> 00:09:08,747
Parsing a program is similar. Take the same line: if x equals equals y then z equals 1, else z equals 2. Its 14 tokens are at the top.

82
00:09:09,197 --> 00:09:14,466
The leaves of the tree are tokens: x, equals equals, y, z, 1, z and 2.

83
00:09:14,916 --> 00:09:17,561
x equals equals y is a relation.

84
00:09:18,011 --> 00:09:20,358
z equals 1 is an assignment.

85
00:09:20,808 --> 00:09:23,560
z equals 2 is another assignment.

86
00:09:24,010 --> 00:09:26,079
The relation is the predicate.

87
00:09:26,529 --> 00:09:29,175
The first assignment is the then statement.

88
00:09:29,625 --> 00:09:32,227
The second assignment is the else statement.

89
00:09:32,677 --> 00:09:36,261
All three together make one if-then-else statement, the root.

90
00:09:36,711 --> 00:09:39,890
Only 7 of the 14 tokens are leaves.

91
00:09:40,340 --> 00:09:49,919
The other 7 tokens are the keywords, the equals signs and the semicolons. They are not leaves. They tell the parser the shape of the tree.

92
00:09:50,369 --> 00:09:59,222
Phase 3. When the structure is known, we try to understand the meaning. Take the sentence: I saw a man on a hill with a telescope.

93
00:09:59,672 --> 00:10:07,181
It looks simple. But it has several meanings. Reading 1: there is a man on a hill, and I watch him with my telescope.

94
00:10:07,631 --> 00:10:11,685
Reading 2: there is a man on a hill, and he has a telescope.

95
00:10:12,135 --> 00:10:16,913
Reading 3: the man is on a hill, and the hill has a telescope on it.

96
00:10:17,363 --> 00:10:21,993
Reading 4: I am on a hill, and I saw a man with my telescope.

97
00:10:22,443 --> 00:10:27,712
The word saw also has more than one meaning: I see, or I cut with a saw.

98
00:10:28,162 --> 00:10:37,378
Understanding the meaning of programs automatically is too hard. So compilers do a limited analysis to catch inconsistencies.

99
00:10:37,828 --> 00:10:48,815
English has another problem. Jack said Jack left his assignment at home. How many Jacks are there? Is Jack talking about himself? Which one left the assignment?

100
00:10:49,265 --> 00:10:58,630
Programming languages define strict rules to avoid this kind of ambiguity. These are variable binding rules, or scoping rules.

101
00:10:59,080 --> 00:11:03,155
In this C plus plus code, there are two variables called Jack.

102
00:11:03,605 --> 00:11:07,637
The second Jack is in an inner scope, shown by the purple box.

103
00:11:08,087 --> 00:11:11,329
The output statement is also in the inner scope.

104
00:11:11,779 --> 00:11:19,225
The rule says: use the nearest definition. So the output uses the inner Jack. The outer Jack is hidden.

105
00:11:19,675 --> 00:11:23,899
The program prints 4. We compiled and ran this code to check it.

106
00:11:24,349 --> 00:11:30,919
Compilers perform many semantic checks besides variable binding. One of them is type checking.

107
00:11:31,369 --> 00:11:34,655
Take the sentence: Jack left her homework at home.

108
00:11:35,105 --> 00:11:42,038
There is a type mismatch between her and Jack. We know they are different people. Presumably Jack is male.

109
00:11:42,488 --> 00:11:45,453
In a program, Jack is declared as an int.

110
00:11:45,903 --> 00:11:50,085
Then the program assigns a string to it. The types do not match.

111
00:11:50,535 --> 00:11:57,383
The C plus plus compiler reports an error and rejects the program. We checked this with the compiler.

112
00:11:57,833 --> 00:12:12,339
Phase 4 is optimization. English has no strong counterpart. It is like editing. The compiler changes the program automatically, so that it runs faster, or uses less memory or less power.

113
00:12:12,789 --> 00:12:14,560
Here is a small function.

114
00:12:15,010 --> 00:12:20,791
The slide example: x equals y times 0 is the same as x equals 0.

115
00:12:21,241 --> 00:12:25,700
x is 0, so the test x equals equals 1 is never true.

116
00:12:26,150 --> 00:12:32,251
The code inside the test never executes. That is dead code, and the compiler removes it.

117
00:12:32,701 --> 00:12:34,728
The result returns 2 directly.

118
00:12:35,178 --> 00:12:42,410
We compiled this function with clang, with optimization on. The output has no test and returns 2.

119
00:12:42,860 --> 00:12:54,721
There are other optimizations: register allocation, cache-aware optimizations, and special-purpose instructions, for example for graphics or the fast Fourier transform.

120
00:12:55,171 --> 00:13:00,483
Can a correct optimization introduce a security problem? The answer is yes.

121
00:13:00,933 --> 00:13:07,568
This function reads star p first. If p is NULL, that read is undefined behaviour in C.

122
00:13:08,018 --> 00:13:19,047
Undefined behaviour gives the optimizer surprising freedom. It may assume that undefined behaviour never happens. So it assumes that p is not NULL.

123
00:13:19,497 --> 00:13:23,871
Then the NULL test can never be true, and the optimizer removes it.

124
00:13:24,321 --> 00:13:32,022
We checked this. Clang with optimization level 2 removes the test. With optimization off, the test stays.

125
00:13:32,472 --> 00:13:41,773
Optimizations preserve defined behaviour, not the intent of the programmer. The programmer wanted a safety check, and it is gone.

126
00:13:42,223 --> 00:13:47,898
Phase 5 is code generation. It translates the program into another language.

127
00:13:48,348 --> 00:13:53,361
It usually produces assembly code, for a special-purpose instruction set.

128
00:13:53,811 --> 00:14:00,169
A good compiler is retargetable: the same compiler can generate code for different hardware.

129
00:14:00,619 --> 00:14:07,531
A compiler does a series of translations between intermediate forms. The first form is the source.

130
00:14:07,981 --> 00:14:09,965
The last form is assembly.

131
00:14:10,415 --> 00:14:19,716
All the forms between them are intermediate languages, internal to the compiler. Sometimes there is only one, called the IR.

132
00:14:20,166 --> 00:14:27,291
The forms are ordered by level of abstraction. The source is the highest level, and assembly is the lowest.

133
00:14:27,741 --> 00:14:33,224
Higher levels keep the high-level meaning of the program, for example loops and types.

134
00:14:33,674 --> 00:14:42,805
Lower levels expose features that higher levels hide, such as registers and memory layout. This enables new optimizations.

135
00:14:43,255 --> 00:14:45,964
But lower levels hide the high-level meaning.

136
00:14:46,414 --> 00:14:59,513
To design an IR that exposes the hardware and also keeps the structure is very hard. The slides show MLIR, a compiler framework with multiple levels of IR.

137
00:14:59,963 --> 00:15:13,829
The overall structure of almost every compiler follows this outline. But the proportions have changed since FORTRAN. In early compilers, lexing and parsing were the most complex and expensive parts.

138
00:15:14,279 --> 00:15:17,991
Today, optimization dominates all the other phases.

139
00:15:18,441 --> 00:15:20,425
Lexing and parsing are cheap.

140
00:15:20,875 --> 00:15:25,206
The bar widths only show this idea. They are not measured values.

141
00:15:25,656 --> 00:15:31,117
Lexing and parsing are cheap today because the theory in this course made them routine.

142
00:15:31,567 --> 00:15:36,943
What is the core of a compiler? It depends on the language and on the application.

143
00:15:37,393 --> 00:15:51,537
For C, C plus plus and other low-level imperative languages, the core is optimization and program analysis: alias analysis, global and link-time optimization, and bug detection.

144
00:15:51,987 --> 00:16:04,019
For Java, JavaScript and other interpreted languages, the core is just-in-time compilation, garbage collection, polymorphism and efficient object orientation.

145
00:16:04,469 --> 00:16:11,915
For TensorFlow and other machine learning languages, the core is the IR, scheduling and parallelization.

146
00:16:12,365 --> 00:16:23,885
ECE 351 compiles a hardware description language. Its core is parsing, optimization, code generation and object-oriented design.

147
00:16:24,335 --> 00:16:30,799
The course notes show the stages of a typical C compiler. The boxes are the kinds of data.

148
00:16:31,249 --> 00:16:33,105
The arrows are the stages.

149
00:16:33,555 --> 00:16:38,867
ECE 351 focuses on the three stages in orange.

150
00:16:39,317 --> 00:16:44,949
Lexing chunks the input into a list of tokens, and usually removes white space.

151
00:16:45,399 --> 00:16:48,065
Parsing builds the AST from the tokens.

152
00:16:48,515 --> 00:16:50,691
Code generation makes the output.

153
00:16:51,141 --> 00:17:01,509
Optimization changes the intermediate language into a better intermediate language. That is also an ECE 351 focus.

154
00:17:01,959 --> 00:17:19,197
Now an exercise. Divide this line into tokens: while x not equals 0 do x equals x minus 1, semicolon. Give the unit of each token: keyword, variable, operator, constant or punctuation. Then count them.

155
00:17:19,647 --> 00:17:21,289
Pause the video and try it.

156
00:17:21,739 --> 00:17:24,662
Read from left to right. while is a keyword.

157
00:17:25,112 --> 00:17:26,669
x is a variable.

158
00:17:27,119 --> 00:17:31,770
Not equals is one operator. It is two characters, but one token.

159
00:17:32,220 --> 00:17:33,948
0 is a constant.

160
00:17:34,398 --> 00:17:35,827
do is a keyword.

161
00:17:36,277 --> 00:17:42,315
Then x equals x minus 1: variable, operator, variable, operator, constant.

162
00:17:42,765 --> 00:17:44,941
The semicolon is punctuation.

163
00:17:45,391 --> 00:17:57,785
In total: 2 keywords, 3 variables, 3 operators, 2 constants and 1 punctuation mark. 2 plus 3 plus 3 plus 2 plus 1 is 11 tokens.

164
00:17:58,235 --> 00:18:02,801
The 9 spaces are white space. The lexer usually removes them.

165
00:18:03,251 --> 00:18:09,693
To summarize. A language has syntax and semantics. Every design choice is a trade-off.

166
00:18:10,143 --> 00:18:18,293
Interpreters run programs as is. Compilers translate them first. Just-in-time compilation mixes both.

167
00:18:18,743 --> 00:18:27,383
A compiler has five phases: lexical analysis, parsing, semantic analysis, optimization and code generation.

168
00:18:27,833 --> 00:18:35,577
Between the phases flow tokens, the AST, the IR, three-address format, and finally the output program.

169
00:18:36,027 --> 00:18:45,051
Optimizations preserve defined behaviour, not intent. Today optimization dominates, and lexing and parsing are cheap.

170
00:18:45,501 --> 00:18:57,255
Many open problems remain, for example parallel code generation, power optimization and better type systems. Next video: lexical analysis in detail.
